1
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:05,719
tremendous crash of glass.

2
00:00:05,719 --> 00:00:09,760
No one stirred in the farmhouse before noon on the following day,

3
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:13,760
and the word went round that from somewhere or other the pigs had acquired

4
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:18,959
the money to buy themselves another case of whiskey.

5
00:00:18,959 --> 00:00:21,719
Chapter 10.

6
00:00:23,079 --> 00:00:26,440
Years passed. The seasons came and went.

7
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:29,719
The short animal lives fled by.

8
00:00:29,719 --> 00:00:34,760
A time came when there was no one who remembered the old days before the rebellion,

9
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:41,360
except Clover, Benjamin, Moses the Raven, and a number of the pigs.

10
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:43,079
Muriel was dead.

11
00:00:43,079 --> 00:00:46,280
Bluebell, Jessie, and Pincher were dead.

12
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:52,639
Jones, too, was dead. He had died in an inebriates' home in another part of the county.

13
00:00:52,639 --> 00:00:54,920
Snowball was forgotten.

14
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:59,760
Boxer was forgotten, except by the few who had known him.

15
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:06,559
Clover was an old stout mare now, stiff in the joints and with a tendency to rheumy eyes.

16
00:01:06,559 --> 00:01:13,520
She was 2 years past the retiring age, but in fact, no animal had ever actually retired.

17
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:17,840
The talk of setting aside a corner of the pasture for superannuated animals

18
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,719
had long since been dropped.

19
00:01:20,719 --> 00:01:25,559
Napoleon was now a mature boar of 24 stone.

20
00:01:25,559 --> 00:01:32,200
Squealer was so fat that he could, with difficulty, see out of his eyes.

21
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:38,920
Only old Benjamin was much the same as ever, except for being a little grayer about the muzzle,

22
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:45,000
and, since Boxer's death, more morose and taciturn than ever.

23
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,520
There were many more creatures on the farm now, though the increase was not so great as

24
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:52,600
had been expected in earlier years.

25
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:57,399
Many animals had been born to him the rebellion was only a dim tradition

26
00:01:57,399 --> 00:02:02,280
passed on by word of mouth, and others had been bought who had never heard

27
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:05,520
mention of such a thing before their arrival.

28
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:09,440
The farm possessed three horses now besides Clover.

29
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:16,920
They were fine, upstanding beasts, willing workers, and good comrades, but very stupid.

30
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:21,200
None of them proved able to learn the alphabet beyond the letter B.

31
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:24,360
They accepted everything that they were told about the rebellion and the

32
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:31,079
principles of animalism, especially from Clover, for whom they had an almost filial respect,

33
00:02:31,079 --> 00:02:34,680
but it was doubtful whether they understood very much of it.

34
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:38,520
The farm was more prosperous now and better organized.

35
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:42,840
It had even been enlarged by two fields, which had been bought from Mr.

36
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:44,600
Pilkington.

37
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:50,520
The windmill had been successfully completed at last, and the farm possessed a threshing

38
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:57,399
machine and a hay elevator of its own, and various new buildings had been added to it.

39
00:02:57,399 --> 00:03:01,120
Whymper had bought himself a dog cart.

40
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:09,200
The windmill, however, had not, after all, been used for generating electrical power.

41
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:16,040
It was used for milling corn and brought in a handsome money profit.

42
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:21,280
The animals were hard at work building yet another windmill.

43
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:27,719
When that one was finished, so it was said, the dynamos would be installed.

44
00:03:27,719 --> 00:03:33,280
But the luxuries of which Snowball had once taught the animals to dream,

45
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:40,280
the stalls with electric light and hot and cold water, and the three-day week,

46
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:42,680
were no longer talked about.

47
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:49,440
Napoleon had denounced such ideas as contrary to the spirit of animalism.

48
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:56,840
"The truest happiness," he said, "lay in working hard and living frugally."

49
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:00,879
Somehow it seemed as though the farm had grown richer without making the animals

50
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:06,520
themselves any richer, except of course for the pigs and the dogs.

51
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:11,680
Perhaps this was partly because there were so many pigs and so many dogs.

52
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:15,799
It was not that these creatures did not work after their fashion.

53
00:04:15,799 --> 00:04:20,559
There was, as Squealer was never tired of explaining, endless work in the

54
00:04:20,559 --> 00:04:23,879
supervision and organization of the farm.

55
00:04:23,879 --> 00:04:29,319
Much of this work was of a kind that the other animals were too ignorant to understand.

56
00:04:29,319 --> 00:04:34,520
For example, Squealer told them that the pigs had to expend enormous labors every

57
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:42,840
day upon mysterious things called files, reports, minutes, and memoranda.

58
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:47,399
These were large sheets of paper which had to be closely covered with writing,

59
00:04:47,399 --> 00:04:51,959
and as soon as they were so covered, they were burnt in the furnace.

60
00:04:51,959 --> 00:04:56,920
"This was of the highest importance for the welfare of the farm," Squealer said,

61
00:04:56,920 --> 00:05:02,559
"but still neither pigs nor dogs produced any food by their own labor,

62
00:05:02,559 --> 00:05:07,719
and there were very many of them, and their appetites were always good."

63
00:05:07,719 --> 00:05:14,360
As for the others, their life, so far as they knew, was as it had always been.

64
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:18,399
They were generally hungry. They slept on straw.

65
00:05:18,399 --> 00:05:20,399
They drank from the pool.

66
00:05:20,399 --> 00:05:22,879
They labored in the fields.

67
00:05:22,879 --> 00:05:29,799
In winter, they were troubled by the cold, and in summer by the flies.

68
00:05:29,799 --> 00:05:34,440
Sometimes the older ones among them racked their dim memories and tried to

69
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:39,120
determine whether in the early days of the rebellion, when Jones's expulsion

70
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:45,079
was still recent, things had been better or worse than now.

71
00:05:45,079 --> 00:05:47,479
They could not remember.

72
00:05:47,479 --> 00:05:51,520
There was nothing with which they could compare their present lives.

73
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:56,239
They had nothing to go upon except Squealer's lists of figures,

74
00:05:56,239 --> 00:06:03,079
which invariably demonstrated that everything was getting better and better.

75
00:06:03,079 --> 00:06:06,600
The animals found the problem insoluble.

76
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:11,600
In any case, they had little time for speculating on such things now.

77
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:16,879
Only old Benjamin professed to remember every detail of his long life

78
00:06:16,879 --> 00:06:24,159
and to know that things never had been nor ever could be much better or much worse.

79
00:06:24,159 --> 00:06:33,600
Hunger, hardship, and disappointment being, so he said, the unalterable law of life.

80
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:37,360
And yet, the animals never gave up hope.

81
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:43,719
More, they never lost, even for an instant, their sense of honor and privilege in

82
00:06:43,719 --> 00:06:46,840
being members of Animal Farm.

83
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:52,520
They were still the only farm in the whole country, in all England,

84
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:56,000
owned and operated by animals.

85
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,840
Not one of them, not even the youngest, not even the newcomers who had been

86
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:07,680
brought from farms 10 or 20 miles away, ever ceased to marvel at that.

87
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:12,440
And when they heard the gun booming and saw the green flag fluttering at the

88
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:17,639
masthead, their hearts swelled with imperishable pride.

89
00:07:17,639 --> 00:07:25,319
And the talk turned always towards the old heroic days, the expulsion of Jones,

90
00:07:25,319 --> 00:07:30,520
the writing of the Seven Commandments, the great battles in which the human

91
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:33,280
invaders had been defeated.

92
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:36,879
None of the old dreams had been abandoned.

93
00:07:36,879 --> 00:07:42,000
The Republic of the animals, which Major had foretold when the green fields of

94
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:47,920
England should be untrodden by human feet was still believed in.

95
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:50,600
Someday it was coming.

96
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:52,600
It might not be soon.

97
00:07:52,600 --> 00:08:00,399
It might not be within the lifetime of any animal now living, but still it was coming.

98
00:08:00,399 --> 00:08:07,479
Even the tune of Beasts of England was perhaps hummed secretly here and there.

99
00:08:07,479 --> 00:08:13,159
At any rate, it was a fact that every animal on the farm knew it, though no

100
00:08:13,159 --> 00:08:16,200
one would have dared to sing it aloud.

101
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:22,639
It might be that their lives were hard and that not all of their hopes had been fulfilled,

102
00:08:22,639 --> 00:08:27,280
but they were conscious that they were not as other animals.

103
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:31,000
If they went hungry, it was not from feeding tyrannical human beings.
